Vercel Speed Insights Implementation
=====================================

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for the Next.js 16.1.6 App Router project.

## What Was Implemented

1. **Package Installation**
   - Installed @vercel/speed-insights version 1.3.1 using pnpm
   - Added as a production dependency in package.json

2. **Integration with Root Layout**
   - Modified: app/layout.tsx
   - Added import: `import { SpeedInsights } from "@vercel/speed-insights/next"`
   - Added `<SpeedInsights />` component inside the `<body>` tag, after the {children} content
   - This follows the recommended pattern for Next.js 13.5+ with App Router

## Why These Changes Were Made

- **Next.js 16.1.6 with App Router**: The project uses Next.js 16.1.6, which is well above version 13.5, so we used the recommended approach for App Router projects by importing from '@vercel/speed-insights/next'
- **Root Layout Placement**: The SpeedInsights component was added to the root layout (app/layout.tsx) to ensure it tracks performance across all pages
- **Inside Body Tag**: Placed the component after {children} within the body tag as recommended by Vercel's documentation for optimal tracking

## Files Changed

1. **app/layout.tsx** - Added SpeedInsights component import and JSX
2. **package.json** - Added @vercel/speed-insights dependency
3. **pnpm-lock.yaml** - Updated with new dependency and its transitive dependencies

## Verification

✅ Build completed successfully (pnpm run build)
✅ TypeScript compilation passed
✅ No new linting errors introduced (existing linting issues in other files were pre-existing)
✅ All static pages generated successfully

## Notes

- The Speed Insights component will automatically collect and send performance metrics to Vercel when deployed
- No additional configuration is required; the component works out of the box
- The integration preserves all existing functionality and code structure
- The pnpm-lock.yaml file was properly updated to reflect the new dependency
